        <div id="is-protein-powder-gluten-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Protein Powder Gluten-Free?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most protein sources are naturally gluten-free. Whey, casein, egg white, pea, brown rice, soy, hemp, pumpkin seed and collagen proteins contain no gluten on their own. Gluten is a protein found in wheat, barley and rye, and related grains such as spelt, so a plain protein powder made from any of the sources above starts out gluten-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            The risk comes from what is added and how the powder is made. Flavorings, inclusions like cookie or brownie pieces, malt-based ingredients, oat flour, some thickeners and greens blends can introduce gluten. So can cross-contact, where a gluten-free powder is made on the same equipment or in the same facility as products containing wheat or barley. That is why two whey powders with the same protein source can differ in their suitability for celiac disease.
          </p>

          <p>
            The result is that "is protein powder gluten-free?" has an unsatisfying answer: usually, but not reliably enough for celiac disease without checking. Plain unflavored isolates and single-ingredient plant proteins are the lowest-risk category. Dessert flavors, mass gainers and meal replacement shakes, which often include oat powder or grain-based carbohydrate, are the highest. Our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">best meal replacement shake</a> guide shows how much more goes into those products.
          </p>

          <p>
            For people without celiac disease or a wheat allergy, small traces rarely matter, and a brand's gluten-free claim is usually enough. For people with celiac disease, where even small amounts of gluten can damage the small intestine, the questions in the next sections are worth asking every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certified-vs-claim" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Certified Gluten-Free vs a Gluten-Free Claim
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the US, a "gluten-free" label is regulated. Under the FDA rule that took effect in 2014, foods labeled gluten-free, no gluten, free of gluten or without gluten must contain less than 20 parts per million of gluten. The rule covers dietary supplements as well as conventional foods, so it applies to protein powder. The 20 ppm threshold matches the international Codex standard and is the level most people with celiac disease tolerate, according to the research the FDA relied on.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is enforcement. The FDA does not require manufacturers to test their products or to use a specific testing method; the company is responsible for making sure the claim is true. In practice, reputable brands test, but there is no routine outside check before a gluten-free claim appears on a tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party certification adds that check. The Gluten-Free Certification Organization, run by the Gluten Intolerance Group and known as GFCO, certifies products at 10 ppm or less, half the FDA threshold, with ongoing product testing and annual facility audits. NSF runs its own gluten-free certification with audits and testing too. A certification seal does not make a product perfectly free of gluten, but it means someone outside the company has inspected the process and tested the product.
          </p>

