            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For celiac disease, certified products are the safest default, and a brand gluten-free claim from a company with a short, simple ingredient list is a reasonable second choice. Products with no gluten statement at all, even if the ingredients look harmless, are the ones to question. Some people also use independent testing services such as Gluten Free Watchdog, which publishes test results for specific products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients-to-watch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ingredients That Can Hide Gluten
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the allergen statement, but do not stop there. US law requires wheat to be declared as a major allergen, so any wheat ingredient should appear in the ingredient list or a "Contains: wheat" statement. Barley, rye and oats are not on that list, which means they do not have to be called out in the allergen statement and can be easy to miss. You have to find them in the ingredient list yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Barley malt is the most common hidden source. Look for malt, malt extract, malt flavoring, malt syrup, malted milk powder and malted barley, which turn up in chocolate, vanilla malt and dessert flavors. Under FDA labeling conventions, "malt" on its own refers to barley malt unless another grain is named. Brewer's yeast, a byproduct of beer brewing, can also carry gluten unless it is labeled gluten-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oats are a judgment call. Oats are naturally gluten-free but are frequently contaminated with wheat or barley during growing and milling, and a small share of people with celiac disease react to avenin, the protein in oats themselves. The FDA allows oats in gluten-free foods if the finished product is under 20 ppm. If you react to oats, avoid powders with oat flour, oat protein or oat milk powder, and check meal shakes especially carefully.
          </p>

          <p>
            Several other ingredients deserve a second look. Cookie, brownie, cake and cereal flavors may contain real wheat-based pieces. Greens blends sometimes include wheatgrass or barley grass; the grass itself does not contain gluten, but seed contamination is possible, so these are acceptable only in a product that is labeled or certified gluten-free. Maltodextrin and dextrose in the US are usually made from corn, and if they are made from wheat, wheat must be declared.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the ingredient list for barley, rye, oats and malt</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The "Contains" allergen line will flag wheat but not barley, rye or oats. Scan the full ingredient list for malt, barley, rye, oats, wheatgrass and cookie or cereal pieces, and prefer a certified gluten-free seal when you have celiac disease.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cross-contact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cross-Contact and Shared Facilities
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even a powder with entirely gluten-free ingredients can pick up gluten during manufacturing. Supplement plants often run many products on the same lines, including mass gainers with oat flour, cookie-flavored proteins and bars. Cleaning between runs reduces the risk, but the residue that remains is exactly what gluten-free testing and certification are designed to catch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Statements like "made in a facility that also processes wheat" are voluntary and not regulated, so their presence or absence tells you less than it seems. A product with such a statement may still test well under 20 ppm, while a product without one may share equipment. A gluten-free claim or certification is far more informative, because it speaks to the finished product rather than the building.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a product you like has no gluten statement, it is reasonable to ask the brand directly whether it tests for gluten, what threshold it uses and whether the line is shared. Brands that test can usually answer quickly. Vague replies are a reason to choose a certified alternative instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins have one extra consideration. Pea and rice proteins are processed from crops that can be grown, harvested or milled near wheat and barley. That is not a reason to avoid them, and both of our plant picks from Garden of Life carry NSF gluten-free certification per the brand, but it is another reason certification matters more than the protein source. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide covers the wider plant category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="celiac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Gluten-Free Protein for Celiac Disease and Sensitivity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Celiac disease is an autoimmune condition in which gluten damages the lining of the small intestine, and it affects roughly 1% of people. If you have it, your doctor or gastroenterologist, ideally with a registered dietitian, should guide how strictly you avoid gluten and which products you trust. A protein powder is a small part of the diet, but because it is often taken daily, it is worth getting right.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you suspect celiac disease but have not been diagnosed, talk to a doctor before cutting out gluten. The standard blood tests and biopsy depend on you still eating gluten, and going gluten-free first can make the diagnosis much harder. Non-celiac gluten sensitivity and wheat allergy are different conditions with different levels of strictness, and only proper testing can tell them apart.
          </p>

          <p>
            People with celiac disease sometimes have other digestive issues, including lactose intolerance while the gut heals. In that case a whey isolate, which is very low in lactose, or a plant protein may be gentler than a whey concentrate. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">sensitive stomach</a> guides cover those choices.
          </p>

          <p>
            For people avoiding gluten by preference rather than medical need, most protein powders will be fine, and a brand's gluten-free claim is plenty. The extra steps in this guide, certification seals, checking for barley malt and asking about shared lines, matter most for celiac disease and wheat allergy, where small exposures have real consequences.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the allergen statement alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                US law requires wheat to be declared, but not barley, rye or oats. A tub can say Contains: milk and still include barley malt. Read the full ingredient list for malt, barley, rye, oats and wheatgrass rather than trusting the allergen line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a brand is gluten-free across the range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gluten claims and certifications apply to specific products, and sometimes specific flavors. A brand's plain vanilla may be certified while its cookie flavor contains wheat pieces. Check the label on every flavor and size before you reorder or switch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating no gluten ingredients as gluten-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powder made only from whey or pea can still pick up gluten on shared equipment. Without a gluten-free claim or certification, nobody has said the finished product is under 20 ppm. For celiac disease, choose products that make the claim or carry a seal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going gluten-free before getting tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you suspect celiac disease, cutting gluten first can make the blood tests and biopsy unreliable. See your doctor while you are still eating gluten, get a proper diagnosis, then use certified products to build a safe routine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting about oats in meal shakes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many meal replacement and weight-gainer powders use oat flour or oat protein. Oats are often cross-contaminated, and some people with celiac disease react to oats themselves. If oats bother you, choose a plain protein powder or a certified gluten-free shake made without them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

